# 1. Is the renewal-chain analysis correct?

Confirmed, step by step, with two sharpenings that change how the "weakest link" rule must be stated.

**"Agent instead of thread changes visibility but not meaning" — correct, and it is stronger than it sounds.** In this architecture the only notion of authorship is *which stream a fact is read from* (source scope, d5). A library thread's renewals were indistinguishable from the holder's own appends — hidden as a separate actor. An agent renewer has an identity, so its renewals are attributable. That is a real difference: the agent case does not *change* what a renewal means; it makes the pre-existing question — *is this the holder?* — visible and unavoidable. The thread version swept it under the rug; the agent version cannot. That is progress, not a defect.

**"Decisive question: unconditional vs on evidence" — correct.** This is the whole question, and it is answered *by reading the renewer's composition*, not by reading the lease facts. An agent that appends a renewal naming a lease while holding no evidence about the holder is Jini's `LeaseRenewalManager` in cleaner materials — cleaner only in the sense that it is now auditable as such.

**"On evidence → renewal chain" — correct in shape, with one precision.** Each link is a *full lease*: the manager grants the worker a lease and judges the worker; the terminal grantor grants the manager a lease and judges the manager. The chain is not a new kind of lease; it is ordinary leases composed, each link its own grantor/possessor pair. "Terminating at whoever is actually doing the work" is right, and it is why the chain adds detection *latency* at every link (each link contributes its own duration plus its own judgment cadence before the next link up can even notice).

**"Weakest link defines the guarantee" — a real rule, but not as phrased.** "Weakest" implies a gradation, and there is none. The behavior is a *cut*, not a weakening. One unconditional renewer anywhere does not degrade the downstream guarantee; it severs it — every renewal downstream of that point is evidence about *the renewer*, not about the worker.

It is also, importantly, not a rule about resource safety. The terminal grantor is never unsafe: it reclaims when the *manager* goes quiet, and that local rule is unchanged by anything below it. What the chain changes is only **what the manager's renewals mean**. The grantor cannot even see the chain — it sees only the manager's renewals. So the weakest-link rule is a rule about the *intermediate renewers' internal discipline*, checkable only by reading their compositions.

State it so an implementer can check it:

> For a chain worker → … → renewer → grantor, define for each link the renewer's predicate *"I append a renewal only while I hold fresh evidence, observed on my own clock, that my immediate holder is still there"* — where the worker's own renewal is its evidence about itself, appended only while it is actually doing the work. The chain's end-to-end meaning — *the grantor's reclaim tracks worker death* — holds only if that predicate is true at **every** link. The check walks the chain and asks, at each link, *"on what evidence does this renewer append its next renewal?"* Any answer weaker than *"fresh evidence about my immediate holder"* is a cut: from there outward, renewals prove only the renewer's liveness.

That is the rule stripped of slogan. It is a conjunction, not a weakest-link; and it is about meaning, not safety.

**Batching and cadence decoupling — I refute one and half-confirm the other (this is the crux of question 2, developed there).**

**"Manager is an interpreter, not a component; dao.lease.md gains no component" — correct.** A manager reads lease facts and appends renewal facts, the same shape as `forward!` and the JIT. It needs no new component. But being interpreter-shaped does not answer the authority question; the orchestrator correctly identifies that the question has no current answer. My answer to it is: in this slice we do not answer it, we decline it (question 3).

# 2. Is delegated renewal worth having at all?

**Not in this slice.** The two claimed benefits do not both require delegation, and the one that does is mostly the defect wearing a use case.

**Batching does not require delegation.** Jini's `LeaseMap` bundles many renewals into one call; in this vocabulary that is simply a renewal fact whose `:dao.lease/lease` key carries a *set* of ids held by the same author from the same grantor. The author is still the holder. That is the useful part of `LeaseMap`, and it costs zero authority machinery — it is a one-line extension of the renewal fact, adopted now. Conflating it with delegation overstates the case for a second principal.

**Cadence decoupling is real but its legitimate case is narrow, and its illegitimate case is exactly the defect.** The honest version of "a holder doing long synchronous work cannot renew tightly" is: the holder is alive, making progress, provably so, but structurally unable to append renewals at the required cadence. In this non-blocking, interpreter-driven architecture, that almost always collapses to *the driver is not running the holder* — which is the wedge/death signal the lease exists to expose. A manager that renews through that silence is manufacturing liveness, the very thing all five reviewers rejected. The residual legitimate case — holder busy but emitting progress — is better served by the holder's own stream carrying those progress facts, and by granting a longer duration, not by a second principal whose authority is undesigned.

Against it, three costs: authority at every link (any link without it is an open door — question 3); **additive detection latency** (worker death becomes reclaimable only after the sum of every link's duration plus cadence); and the guarantee at stake is only *meaning*, never *safety* — which is to say the chain buys nothing the terminal grantor didn't already have locally, and spends undesigned machinery to buy it.

Verdict: adopt batching; **defer delegated renewal with an explicit denial** in the document.

# 3. Does delegated renewal require Shibi?

**No.** And the weakest sufficient mechanism is, for what is worth having, *no mechanism at all*.

The ladder, weakest first:

1. **First-person renewal (adopted).** The holder renews; a helper may be composed into the holder's own writer, where it writes *as the holder*. That is delegation of labor, not of authority, and it is already covered by "a holder renews from its own control flow." No authority mechanism, because no second identity exists.
2. **Batching (adopted).** A renewal naming a set of the author's own leases. Also no authority mechanism.
3. **Named-principal authorization (deferred, and this is the weakest sufficient mechanism *if* a separate-identity renewer is ever required).** The holder appends a grantor-visible fact naming a specific renewer: `{:dao.lease/event :dao.lease/authorize :dao.lease/lease <id> :dao.lease/renewer <agent-id>}` (or the grant records it), and the judge accepts renewals only from the holder or a named renewer, per the holder's own stream. That is plain data — scoped to one lease, revocable by a later holder fact, lapsing with the lease's own tenure — and it composes along the chain because each link's grantor reads the authorizing fact from the *holder's own source*. It is not Shibi: it is not bearer, not attenuable, not offline-verifiable. It is exactly sufficient and nothing more.
4. **Full Shibi capability token — strictly stronger than needed.** Bearer transfer, attenuation chains, offline verification, caveat chains: none of them are required to express "this principal may renew this lease until it lapses." They are answers to questions this slice does not ask.
5. **No authority at all — insufficient, and name the cost.** If the grantor accepts any renewal naming the lease, the lease stops being evidence about the holder and becomes evidence about *whoever can append to the renewal channel*. That is fine only for a lease whose subject harms no one but the holder (a paused forwarder); for any scarce subject it is an open door, and in every case it silently severs the chain at the very first delegation link. It is not a weaker mechanism; it is the absence of one.

**The answer:** keep renewal first-person; take batching; if a separate-identity renewer is ever needed, use the holder's named-principal authorization fact. Shibi is not required for any of it.

# 4. Do leases force the capability-or-currency decision?

**They must stay out of it; at most they add evidence, and they settle nothing now.**

The discovery document's precondition fires only when a design makes Shibi *load-bearing*. This document, on my recommendation, does not: delegated renewal is deferred, and its fallback (named-principal facts, or nothing) needs no Shibi. So leases do not trigger the precondition, and they do not depend on the unresolved decision.

The *physics* of a lease caveat — time-bounded, scoped to one lease, revocable — are squarely capability-physics, not currency-physics: nothing about "this principal may renew this lease until it lapses" is fungible or needs double-spend resolution. So **if and when** delegation ever lands via a token, that use case is evidence for the *capability* reading and against the *currency* reading. But one capability-shaped consumer does not settle what Shibi *is* project-wide; the discovery document's warning is precisely that designs must not smuggle in an answer by depending on one. The forcing function stays where it was put: any future design that would make Shibi load-bearing must first force the decision. `dao.lease.md`'s job is to be correct either way — which is what the deferred text below achieves.

# 5. What `dao.lease.md` should say now

The device that lets the document stand without Shibi and without a future rewrite: state the judge's acceptance rule in first-person terms now, and state that when delegation arrives, *only the acceptance rule widens* — the renewal fact and the lapse discipline do not change.

Proposed changes to the current draft, in document order.

**A.** Amend the renewal fact to admit batching:

> A renewal carries no time and proposes nothing. It says one thing — *I still want this* — and the fact of it, that it was appended and when it is observed, is its entire content. The `:dao.lease/lease` key may carry one id or a set of ids, all held by the author from the same grantor; a set is one renewal for many leases — the useful part of Jini's `LeaseMap` — and it is not delegation, because the author is still the holder. A renewal that asked for a new duration would be negotiating; renegotiation, where a composition wants it, is a new proposal, not a fat renewal.

**B.** Insert a new section, after the renewal fact and before "Expiry is an interpretation":

> ## Who may renew
>
> A renewal is evidence about its author. The grant names the holder, and the holder's own renewal is the only renewal that is evidence about the holder. A judge accepts a renewal as renewing a lease only when the fact is authored by the holder — recognized as the holder exactly as any author is recognized, by the source the fact is read from. A fact authored by any other principal, however it reads, is not a renewal: an agent that appends "the holder is still here" is making a claim about someone else, and a grantor has no more reason to believe that claim than any other unbacked fact. Renewal by another is delegation, and delegation needs authority; this vocabulary has none, so it has no delegated renewal.
>
> That is the general form of the rule already stated against Jini's `LeaseRenewalManager`. It does not matter whether the renewer is a background thread, a library helper, or another agent: a renewer that is not the holder either renews unconditionally — manufacturing false liveness for a holder that may be wedged — or renews on evidence of the holder, which is a renewal chain and requires the authority this document defers. A helper composed into the holder's own writer is not a second party; it writes as the holder, and its renewal is the holder's. What is forbidden is a renewal whose author is not the holder.
>
> Delegated renewal is therefore deferred, not designed. When a composition demonstrates the need — a holder alive but provably unable to renew at the required cadence, and a second principal better placed to attest to it — the authority for it will be a fact on the holder's own stream naming a specific renewer, or whatever authorization mechanism then exists. This document will not change when that lands: the renewal fact and the lapse discipline stand as they are, and only the judge's rule for whose renewals it accepts widens.
>
> Batching needs no delegation and is not deferred; it is the set-renewal above, with the author still the holder.

**C.** Replace the machinery line in "What a lease may never do" (the current draft's "It is never renewed by machinery…") with the principal-neutral form:

> It is never renewed by any principal other than the holder, however that renewer is realized — thread, library helper, or agent — because renewal is evidence about its author, and only the holder's own renewal is evidence about the holder. A renewer that is not the holder either renews unconditionally, which is Jini's `LeaseRenewalManager` wearing a different shape, or renews on evidence of the holder, which is delegated renewal and is deferred (see Who may renew). A holder renews from its own control flow, at a cadence its driver chooses; a helper composed into that control flow writes as the holder and is not a second party.

**D.** Amend "Out of scope":

> Delegated renewal — one agent renewing on another's behalf — is deferred until a demonstrated need and an authority mechanism, neither of which this document assumes (see Who may renew). Transfer of a lease between holders likewise waits for a real need. Authorization, when delegation arrives, is the then-current authority mechanism; Shibi is reserved for it, but this document does not make Shibi load-bearing, and until such a mechanism exists no renewal may be authored by anyone but the holder. A renewal service that renews on a holder's behalf unconditionally is forbidden above, not deferred.

The one sentence that does the future-proofing work, worth calling out: *"the renewal fact and the lapse discipline stand as they are, and only the judge's rule for whose renewals it accepts widens."* That is the seam. Shibi, or a plain named-principal fact, or something else entirely, slots into the acceptance rule without touching the vocabulary's core.

*(Propose only — no edits made.)*
